$DIR/main.p:15:10: shared is read here while the task started at main.p:14 may write it
    The task started at main.p:14 writes the memory allocated at main.p:12 at
    main.p:9, in touch called at main.p:14, and s waits for it at main.p:16.
    Meanwhile shared is read at main.p:15 in main, so the two may run at the
    same time, one of them writing: a data race.
    shared and the memory allocated at main.p:12 are reached through different
    views, which may point to the same memory: the compiler cannot tell where
    the memory they look at comes from, and both may hold values of type int.
    Two slices of one type are accepted when the task uses each only as x[i],
    with the same loop variable i: the program then checks, before each task
    starts, that the slices start at the same element or do not overlap.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks
    run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data
    races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Wait for the tasks before using shared:
        s.Wait()
    Or lock one sync.Mutex around every use of it, in the tasks and here:
        mu.Lock()
        ...
        mu.Unlock()
    (see "Scoped tasks" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3402" for a longer explanation)
